## secure-chat onion service (added 2026-07-28)
##
## This daemon exists to publish ONE onion service. It is not a client proxy
## and not a relay, so everything that would make it either is switched off.
## Overrides the package defaults in
## /usr/share/tor/tor-service-defaults-torrc (torrc wins over that file).
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# No SOCKS. The package default opens 127.0.0.1:9050 AND a WORLD-WRITABLE unix
# socket at /run/tor/socks; neither is needed to publish a service, and both
# would let any local process route traffic through Tor. 0 disables both.
SOCKSPort 0

# Never act as a relay, directory mirror, or exit.
ORPort 0
DirPort 0
ExitRelay 0

# Metadata minimization (I2). The relay itself writes no access log; Tor must
# not reintroduce one. SafeLogging scrubs addresses, and warn drops the routine
# notice-level chatter so a seized host yields no who-connected-when trail.
SafeLogging 1
Log warn syslog

# Pentest 2026-07-29 L-7 — the two lines above do NOT make this host log-free,
# and the I2 claim was overstated. Tor keeps /var/lib/tor/secure-chat/../state,
# which records BWHistory*Values (bandwidth in fixed time buckets — i.e. a
# service-activity timeline) and EntryGuard entries with first-seen timestamps.
# Neither SafeLogging nor the log level touches that file; it is how Tor works.
# AvoidDiskWrites only makes the flushes less frequent, it does not stop them.
# Accepted, and written down rather than claimed away: a seized host yields a
# coarse "the service was busy at these times" trail, but no per-client data.
# Reasoned from Tor's documented state format, not reproduced here.
AvoidDiskWrites 1

# The service. Tor terminates the onion protocol and forwards plaintext HTTP to
# the loopback uvicorn — the same socket Caddy proxies to, so both front ends
# share one process (no split-brain rooms). uvicorn itself stays bound to
# 127.0.0.1 and is never exposed on a public interface.
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/secure-chat/
HiddenServiceVersion 3
HiddenServicePort 80 127.0.0.1:8000

# Proof-of-work defence against INTRODUCTION floods (tor >= 0.4.8). Keep it on,
# but be precise about what it buys: it prices the introduction handshake, i.e.
# building circuits. It does nothing about the attack that actually matters
# here (pentest 2026-07-29 M-2), which builds ONE circuit and then sends cheap
# HTTP on it — ~2 req/s against /api/auth/challenge denies login to every
# existing account, because SECURE_CHAT_TRUSTED_PROXIES is deliberately unset
# and that limiter is one global bucket. deploy/README.md used to credit PoW
# with blunting exactly that; it does not.
HiddenServicePoWDefensesEnabled 1

# What DOES apply to the M-2 attack: bound how much one circuit can do. A
# request flood down a single circuit now hits a stream cap and the circuit is
# torn down, so the attacker has to keep paying the PoW-priced introduction
# cost above to continue. 24 concurrent streams is comfortably above what the
# client opens (one WebSocket plus a handful of /api fetches) while being far
# below what a flood needs.
# This raises the price of the DoS; it does not remove it. Still accepted.
HiddenServiceMaxStreams 24
HiddenServiceMaxStreamsCloseCircuit 1
